The fourteen PPC destination-mask operators belong to `crand`, `crandc`, `creqv`, `crnand`, `crnor`, `cror`, and `crorc`. Their fourteen **source-reading** shifts are safe and counted separately below.

For MIPS RORV, converting an unrestricted unsigned register value to `int` is implementation-defined when it cannot be represented. On the usual 32-bit conversion, `sa` spans the entire signed range. Even restricting attention to representable, nonnegative register values leaves oversized counts. This is not merely the zero-count defect in immediate ROR.

The DEC21143 finding survives checking the state machine: the read-direction branch can `break` before the end-of-data transition, while the common `mii_bit++` still executes. Its sibling SROM counter really does have an effective bound.

**Configuration-dependent and host-only findings**

These are not ranked as ordinary guest-controlled count selection. A guest can execute the eventual device/network operation, but configuration supplies the hazardous range.

| File; grep anchor | N | Count → range | Left type | Reachability and forms |
|---|---:|---|---|---|
| `src/net/net_ip.c`; `x |= (1 << xl) - 1` | 1 | `32-prefix_length` → **2…31** | I | Configuration enables **c** with `/1`; network processing evaluates it |
| `src/devices/dev_dec5800.c`; `1 << cpu->machine->ncpus` | 2 | CLI permits **1…256** | I | **c** at 31, **b** from 32 |
| `src/devices/dev_sgi_ip19.c`; same anchor | 1 | Same | I | **c, b**; the following literal-count shift has another signed-overflow concern |
| `src/devices/dev_dec5800.c`; `1 << interrupt->line` | 2 | Registered **0…31**; stock connection uses 28 | I | **c** requires additional configured wiring |
| `src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c`; `uint32_t line = 1 << interrupt->line` | 2 | Registered **0…31**; stock MACE connections avoid 31 | I | **c** requires additional configured wiring |
| `src/devices/dev_vr41xx.c`; `d->giuint` | 2 | GIU registration **0…31**; stock PCIC connection uses 9 | I | **c** requires additional configured wiring |
| `src/devices/dev_kn02ba.c`; `devinit->interrupt_path, 1 << i` | 1 | Initialization loop **0…31** | I | **c**, initialization |
| `src/console/console.c`; `int mask = 1 << (3-button)` | 1 | X button numbers 1…255 give **−252…2** | I | **a**, host input |

The mouse row does not fit your three proposed populations: it is **host GUI input**, rather than guest input, initialization, CLI, or debugger input. `x11.c` passes button events through; the “button 1/2/3” comment is not validation.

The configuration parser also assigns `ncpus` using `atoi`; the CLI’s `[1,256]` check is not a tree-wide validation of that member.

Thus the **57 confirmed operators** divide into:

| Hazardous operand provenance | Operators |
|---|---:|
| Guest instruction/device state without special hazardous configuration | **45** |
| Configuration-dependent | **10** |
| Initialization alone | **1** |
| Host GUI input | **1** |

Configuration-dependent callback rows are **not globally dead**: `emul_parse.c` accepts additional devices and `emul_machine_setup()` passes them to `device_add()`.

**Enumeration of the field cases**

These counts come from enumerating the decoded integer fields, without executing emulator instructions.

| Case | Enumerated domain | Result |
|---|---|---|
| MIPS EXT | 32 × 32 field pairs | **496 negative counts**, 528 nonnegative |
| Ordinary MIPS DEXT | 32 × 32 | Counts **1…63**; **zero hazardous pairs** |
| DEXTM | 32 × 32, with decoder’s `msbd += 32` | **496 wrapped oversized counts** |
| DEXTU | 32 × 32, with decoder’s `lsb += 32` | **496 wrapped oversized counts** |
| Combined DEXT-family decoding | **3,072**, not 4,096 combinations | **992 hazardous combinations** |
| ARM BFI | 32 × 32 | **465 negative**, **31 zero**, **525 counts 1…30**, **2 counts 31**, **1 count 32** |
| MIPS INS | 32 × 32 | Both middle counts are **0…31** for all pairs |
| SGI RE stipple bounds | 32 × 32 | **496 negative** rotation counts |

BFI has **496 reversed-field encodings**, but only **465 negative shift counts**. The other 31 produce width zero. Mixing these two totals would be an enumeration error.

**Priority, kept on the requested two axes**

| Site | CRITICAL under your definition | EASY |
|---|---|---|
| ARM BFI | **Yes.** A valid full-width operation encounters the bad shift. A count-masking host lowering can make its mask zero, preserving the old destination instead of inserting the source. This is a static consequence, not a measured run here. | **Yes**, approximately 3–5 lines |
| MIPS EXT | **Not established as an architectural-result violation.** The hazardous field combinations are architecturally UNPREDICTABLE. Host UB remains a real defect. | **Yes**, bounds check and decline |
| MIPS DEXTM/DEXTU | Same qualification | **Yes**, bounds check after variant adjustment |
| MIPS ROR/RORV | Valid instructions expose the defects; wrong-result behavior remains host/compiler dependent in this read-only review | **Yes**, mask variable count and guard zero |
| Signed-mask rows | Established C99 defects; I would not assign CRITICAL solely from `1 << 31` | Usually **yes per handler** |
| PPC `rldicl`, discussed below | **Yes**, definite wrong architectural result independent of shift UB | **Yes**, one-line correction |

The MIPS manuals explicitly classify the excessive EXT-family field ranges as UNPREDICTABLE. Declining them is a sound emulator policy; claiming the architecture mandates a particular ordinary result or necessarily mandates Reserved Instruction would overstate the evidence. [MIPS64 instruction manual](https://s3-eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/downloads-mips/documents/MD00087-2B-MIPS64BIS-AFP-05.04.pdf)

For **EASY-only priority**, I would put the network mask, M88K rotate mask, and MIPS FCC mask near the front. The fourteen PPC CR edits are individually small, but the whole family is more than a five-line change.

**Smallest honest fixes for the top three**

| Site | Fix |
|---|---|
| [ARM BFI](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c), anchor `X(bfi)` | Reject `msb < lsb` through the decoder’s existing decline path. For the remaining width **1…32**, construct the mask using an explicitly 32-bit all-ones value shifted right by `32-width`. This handles width 32 without shifting by 32 and removes signed-mask overflow. |
| [MIPS EXT](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_mips_instr.c), anchor `SPECIAL3_EXT` | Before selecting/executing `ext`, validate `lsb + msbd < 32`. Otherwise select the existing reserved/decline path. Retain the extraction arithmetic for accepted fields. |
| Same file, anchor `SPECIAL3_DEXTM` | Apply the decoder’s variant adjustments first, then validate `lsb + msbd < 64`; decline otherwise. The cast on the count supplies no protection. |

ARM BFI permits insertion covering the entire 32-bit destination. Therefore rejecting width 32 would discard a valid operation. [ARMv7 architecture manual, BFI](https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5f8daeb7f86e16515cdb8c4e)

For the plain-one masks elsewhere, cast **before** shifting. Where a mask is subsequently retained in a variable, give that variable the intended unsigned type too—for example, MIPS FCC’s `bit`. Replacing the source literal while retaining a narrowing signed temporary is an incomplete portability repair.

